# Cursor AI Rules for OpenClaw Project

## General Principles
- Follow the principle of least surprise
- Make minimal, surgical changes to accomplish tasks
- Preserve existing code patterns and conventions
- Ask for clarification when requirements are ambiguous

## Code Style
- Use consistent indentation and formatting
- Follow language-specific best practices
- Keep line length reasonable (typically 80-100 characters)
- Use meaningful variable and function names

## Testing
- Write tests alongside new code
- Maintain or improve test coverage
- Use descriptive test names that explain what is being tested

## Documentation
- Document public APIs and interfaces
- Keep inline comments focused on "why" not "what"
- Update documentation when changing functionality
- Include usage examples for complex features

## Security
- Never commit sensitive information (API keys, passwords, tokens)
- Validate all inputs
- Use secure coding practices
- Follow the principle of least privilege

## Git Workflow
- Make focused, atomic commits
- Write commit messages in present tense
- Keep commits related to a single logical change
- Avoid committing generated files or build artifacts

## Performance
- Consider performance implications of changes
- Avoid premature optimization
- Profile before optimizing
- Document performance-critical sections

## Refactoring
- Only refactor when explicitly requested
- Ensure tests pass before and after refactoring
- Make refactoring changes separate from feature changes
- Keep refactoring scope limited and focused
